tmux reduced to browser-style tabs.
run many coding agents at once and keep them alive across ssh disconnects.

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 1 shell   2 codex   3 pi  [4 claude]     ^T new  ^W close  ^← ^→ │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ ╭─ Claude Code ────────────────────────────────────────────────╮ │
│ │                                                              │ │
│ │                     Welcome back Yahya!                      │ │
│ │                                                              │ │
│ │                           ▐▛███▜▌                            │ │
│ │                          ▝▜█████▛▘                           │ │
│ │                            ▘▘ ▝▝                             │ │
│ │                                                              │ │
│ │               Opus 5 (1M context) with xhig…                 │ │
│ │                         Claude Max                           │ │
│ │                            ~/dev                             │ │
│ │                                                              │ │
│ ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ │
│                                                                  │
│ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│ ❯ Try "how does tmux work?"                                      │
│ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│   ⏵⏵ bypass permissions on (shift+tab to cycle) · ← for agents   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘


keys:

    ctrl+t (also: ctrl+n)        new tab (claude / codex / pi / opencode / shell)
    ctrl+w                       close tab
    ctrl+→/← (also: ctrl+f/b)    next / previous tab
    ctrl+space                   resync size to the terminal (from a shell tab)


stuck at a fixed width?

ssh sends the window size once, at connect, and relies on SIGWINCH for every
resize after that. across nested ssh hops that signal sometimes never arrives,
so every layer stays pinned at the connect-time size and tabs look frozen no
matter how you drag the window. ctrl+space asks the terminal how big it really
is and applies the answer to every tab. it has to read a reply from the
terminal, so press it in a shell tab, not in an agent tab.


works on mac and linux.
requires a bash and tmux ≥ 3.0. install:

    $ curl -o ~/.local/bin/tabs https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sueszli/tmux-tabs/master/tmux-tabs.sh
    $ chmod +x ~/.local/bin/tabs
    $ tabs
